LaTeXbangla 1.1 — Writing Bangla with ordinary LaTeX

Write Bangla documents with one font and familiar LaTeX commands. LaTeXbangla
switches automatically between Bangla and English, preserving your English
fonts. It supports bold and italic, Bangla lists and numbering, references,
citations, and Bangla text and digits in mathematics.

Getting started

Compile with XeLaTeX or LuaLaTeX. Tested on TeX Live 2026.
For a local installation, copy latexbangla.sty, latexbangla-bengali.lbx and
the code/ folder beside your document. Install one of the supported Bangla
fonts, then use its name below:

    \documentclass{article}
    \usepackage{latexbangla}
    \setbanglamainfont{Kalpurush}
    \begin{document}
    \section{বাংলা এবং English}
    বাংলা and English একই অনুচ্ছেদে।
    \textbf{বাংলা and English দুটোই bold।}
    \end{document}

Supported fonts: Kalpurush, SolaimanLipi, Siyam Rupali, Noto Serif Bengali,
Noto Sans Bengali, Anek Bangla and FreeSerif. Fonts are not included.
For Anek, rename the downloaded variable font to AnekBangla-VF.ttf,
put it beside your document, and use
\setbanglamainfont{AnekBangla-VF.ttf}.

Choosing an English font is optional: use \setmainfont as usual if you want
to change it. Automatic numbers use Bangla by default; set the package option
changecounternumbering=false to keep the usual numbering. Digits you type
retain their script.

Read latexbangla.pdf for the full guide in English and Bangla, with code and
rendered examples, font settings, custom counters and help with older documents.
More examples and bug reports: https://github.com/Neehan/LaTeXbangla

